    "title": "Dodgers Fans in Southern California Can Now Stream Local Games Without Cable",
    "text": "Dodgers fans and supporters of 19 other Major League Baseball clubs will now be able to stream their local teams’ games without a cable subscription.“Each Club’s in-market direct-to-consumer streaming option delivers all local games to subscribers located anywhere in the Club’s home territory,” the MLB said in a press release. “The service essentially removes local blackouts for these Clubs.” Aug 8, 2025; Los Angeles, California, USA; Los Angeles Dodgers shortstop Mookie Betts (50) runs the bases after hitting a home run in the fifth inning as Toronto Blue Jays first baseman Vladimir Guerrero Jr. (27) reacts at Dodger Stadium. Mandatory Credit: Kirby Lee-Imagn ImagesThe Dodgers’ local broadcast rights are currently controlled by Spectrum, and the streaming is being handled through SNLA+, the company’s Spectrum SportsNet LA digital service.For most of the 20 teams now offering streaming, production is handled by MLB and costs about $20 per month or $100 for the season.Dodgers’ Streaming Rights are a Little DifferentFor the Dodgers and five other teams, however, streaming is handled by the clubs’ regional sports networks — Spectrum, in this case — and their prices are different.Streaming the Dodgers will cost you about $30 per month or $200 per year. It’s important to note that the included games are not those that are exclusively broadcast nationally on channels like ESPN and NBC, or streamed on services like Apple TV and Peacock.“We are pleased to continue expanding MLB’s local media services in 2026, ensuring that fans in each location will be able to enjoy another memorable baseball season on the platform of their choice,” said Noah Garden, MLB deputy commissioner of business and media. “We are proud of the award-winning production MLB has offered Clubs while increasing the reach of the games, enhancing production features, and offering greater access to the players and game. Additionally, we are listening to our fans who want blackouts eliminated. MLB’s in-market streaming option allows us to remove a point of friction for the fans.”Reddit user cprice1989 detailed the blackout areas for the Dodgers and the 29 other MLB teams. Click here to check whether your area is eligible for the local streaming package. If you’re outside the Southern California market, games are available to stream on MLB.TV, which will be available on the ESPN app this season. That streaming package can be packaged with an ESPN Unlimited subscription for a discount.",
